On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 13:29:52 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> I want to come up with a good way of documenting this monorepo. Look at
> other significant monorepo projects and see how they do it.

I surveyed eight large repos. The aim was to find out how each one
decides where a doc goes and how each one stops docs drifting away
from the code. Rendering is out of scope here, since
doc/llm-research/doc-site-for-monorepo.org already covers it (Hugo,
ox-hugo, mdoc). Below, each project gets a short note on what it
does, then I list the patterns they share, how our tree compares,
and what I would do first.

* What they do

- FreeBSD. The top-level map is hier(7), a man page that names every
  directory and what it is for. Each tool's reference is a man page
  that sits next to its source and changes in the same commit. The
  long-form docs (Handbook, articles, books) live in a separate doc
  tree, sorted by type rather than by subsystem. Process lives in the
  Committer's Guide, not in tooling config.

- Linux. Documentation/ has been split into "books" written for
  different readers: process/ (how to contribute), admin-guide/,
  dev-tools/, core-api/, and per-subsystem dirs. API reference is
  pulled out of kernel-doc comments in the source. MAINTAINERS maps
  each path to its owner and gives it a status line ("S: Maintained",
  "Odd Fixes", "Orphan", "Obsolete").

- PostgreSQL. The user manual sits in doc/src/sgml. Design docs are
  plain README files inside the code they describe, e.g.
  src/backend/access/nbtree/README. Each one explains the algorithm
  and its invariants for the next person to touch that code. Those
  files are among the most respected docs in open source, and they
  are not rendered anywhere.

- git. Documentation/ holds the man pages (asciidoc). Next to them
  are SubmittingPatches and MyFirstContribution for contributors,
  howto/ for task guides, technical/ for design, and RelNotes/. Tools
  and reviewers point at those files rather than restate the rules.

- Google (g3doc) and Chromium. Docs live in the directory of the code
  they describe and are reviewed in the same change. Every directory
  is expected to have a README.md that says what it is and where to
  start. Chromium adds a top-level docs/ for cross-cutting material,
  with docs/README.md as the index. It also has OWNERS and
  DIR_METADATA files for machine-readable ownership. "Software
  Engineering at Google" (ch. 10) says why: a doc kept away from its
  code goes stale, and stale docs are worse than none.

- LLVM. It has one Sphinx tree per subproject (llvm/docs, clang/docs,
  lld/docs) and a shared Developer Policy. Each subproject documents
  itself, and the policy is written once.

- rust-analyzer. It popularised ARCHITECTURE.md, one short file that
  gives a codemap ("where is the thing that does X") and the
  invariants, and leaves out details that change often. Rust itself
  keeps contributor internals in a separate rustc-dev-guide.

- Django and Ubuntu use Diataxis, which sorts every doc into one of
  four kinds: tutorial, how-to guide, reference, or explanation. It
  gives a fixed answer to "where does this go", which is the question
  a monorepo keeps asking.

* What they have in common

1. A single map at the root. Every project has one entry point that
   names every top-level area in a line or two: hier(7), Chromium's
   docs/README.md, ARCHITECTURE.md.

2. Reference docs live next to the code and change in the same
   commit (man pages, kernel-doc, g3doc, the PostgreSQL READMEs). This
   is the rule that does the most to stop drift.

3. Process docs are written once and everything else points at them:
   the Committer's Guide, Documentation/process, SubmittingPatches,
   LLVM's Developer Policy.

4. Every area has an owner and a status, so a reader knows whether it
   is alive (MAINTAINERS S: lines, OWNERS, DIR_METADATA).

5. Docs are sorted by kind (guide, reference, design), not by the
   date they were written. Personal notes and task lists are not part
   of the tree's documentation.

6. Something checks the docs: kernel "make htmldocs" warnings,
   Chromium presubmits, mandoc -Tlint. The checks are mechanical and
   cheap.

None of these projects needs many small files to work. Most of them
use one README per area plus a few large books, which fits what you
said in "[RFC] how to spec work in a way that's readable for humans"
[1].

* How our tree compares

- No map. README.md lists three of about fifteen top-level
  directories (src, oss, libexec) and describes "just dev".

- No per-project convention. Most of src/ has no README. Where one
  exists it is .md in some projects and .org in others. Specs are
  spec.org, report.org, lore.org or decisions.org, depending on the
  project. Six tools have mdoc pages, which is the FreeBSD pattern and
  already works.

- CLAUDE.md is the most complete process doc in the tree. That is the
  inverse of git and Linux, where process lives in a doc and the tools
  cite it. You started fixing this in "[RFC] commit style" [2] with
  doc/style/commit.md.

- Docs drift. doc/mail-workflow.md is stale, and a zib named a recipe
  that does not exist, as found in "[doc] Need a guide for basic
  operation of the patch workflow" [3].

- Notes and docs are mixed together. doc/ holds tasks.org,
  toread.org and fieldstones.org next to style/ and proposals/. The
  root has todo.org, TODO.md and a second fieldstones.org.

- zib/ is your notebook, which is explanation in Diataxis terms.
  CLAUDE.md treats zib/patch-review-workflow.org as the authority on
  the workflow, but a notebook is not something the code is held to.

* What I would do

These are ordered by value for effort. Each one is a small patch.

1. Rewrite README.md as the map, hier(7)-style: one line for each
   top-level directory, plus pointers to the doc categories below.
   This is the cheapest step and the one with the most effect.

2. Require a README in every src/<project>, with a fixed short
   skeleton: what it is, status (active, experimental, dormant, dead,
   after MAINTAINERS' S: field), how to build and run it, and where
   its spec and man page are. A PostgreSQL-style design section goes
   in the same file only if the project needs one. We should pick one
   format. I would choose org, since specs and zibs already use it and
   Hugo renders it.

3. Give doc/ a Diataxis layout and nothing else: doc/guides/ (how-to),
   doc/process/ and doc/style/ (written once, cited by CLAUDE.md and
   prompts), and doc/design/ for settled explanation that has been
   promoted out of zib/. Reference stays in man pages and READMEs next
   to the code. Personal notes and task lists move out of doc/.

4. Make CLAUDE.md thin. Keep the agent-specific rules and replace the
   process prose with pointers into doc/process/. The nested CLAUDE.md
   files work the same way as the AGENTS.md convention, so they need
   no change.

5. Docs change with code. A patch that changes a tool's behaviour
   also updates its README or man page, and review checks for it.

6. Add a cheap check, e.g. a libexec script that lists any src/*
   without a README, any top-level directory missing from the map, and
   any mandoc -Tlint warnings. It reports from the filesystem, which
   fits "Own the loop".

If you agree with the direction, tell me which steps you want. I
would start with 1 and 2 as one series, since they are small and
make the rest easy to see. Step 3 moves files, so I would rather you
choose the directory names before I post it.

